An index fossil is the preserved remain or trace of an organism used to identify and correlate rock layers of the same relative age across different locations. Though the organism itself vanished long ago, its brief mark can still hold distant strata together.

A useful index fossil should:

- have lived during a short, well-defined span of geologic time, so its presence indicates a narrow age range;
- have been geographically widespread, allowing comparison of layers far apart;
- have been abundant, leaving enough specimens to be found;
- be distinctive and easy to recognize;
- be well preserved in many environments.

Thus, when matching index fossils occur in separate rock layers, those layers are considered roughly the same age—small surviving records of lives that did not last.

“Separation of powers” means dividing government authority among distinct branches, usually:

- the legislature, which makes laws;
- the executive, which carries them out;
- the judiciary, which interprets them.

Each branch has its own duties, yet each can restrain the others through “checks and balances.” A president may veto a bill; legislators may override that veto or remove officials; courts may strike down unconstitutional acts.

The system is used to prevent too much power from settling in one place, as power so often does when safeguards fade. It also promotes accountability, protects individual rights, and encourages careful decisions. The process can be slow and wearying, but that friction is deliberate—a barrier against abuses that can leave institutions, and freedoms, diminished beyond repair.
